raptormark: name every lifted function by its guest address.

Two paths in SetELFData build a name by hand instead of going through
GetUniqueLiftedFuncName, which every symbol-derived function uses and which
produces `<sym>_____<hex>`: the PLT stubs (`fn_plt_<hex>`) and the `main` wrapper
(`__wrap_main`). AddRestDisasmFunc already goes through it. After this change
every function the lifter emits from guest code carries its address, with no
exceptions -- which is what lets a downstream tool tell guest code from remill's
own code by the name alone.

builder/namespace-object needs exactly that. It decides which definitions may
carry ONE name across the programs of a closure by reading the `_____<hex>`
address out of the name and comparing it against the closure's first library
base. The two hand-named paths were invisible to it and were tagged per program.

The two cases pull in opposite directions, which is why both matter:

  - PLT stubs. Measured on Debian /bin/echo and /bin/cat fused with a shared
    library layout, 442 of the 1002 stub references are at LIBRARY addresses:
    identical code at a closure-fixed address in both programs, kept apart by the
    name alone. They reached 89 of 90 partitions, the widest single obstacle to
    sharing a compiled partition. Named by address, they share.

  - __wrap_main. This one wraps the guest's own `main`, so its body differs in
    every program, and it must NOT share. Bare, it is a function with no address
    in its name; remill's `extern "C"` glue is the only other source of such
    functions and that glue IS program-independent, so a rule keyed on "function
    without an address" would have shared two different bodies under one name.
    Named by address it lands below the closure's first library base and is kept
    per program, as it must be.

elfconv's own runtime recognises stubs with strncmp(name, "fn_plt", 6)
(runtime/VmIntrinsics.cpp), which `fn_plt_____<hex>` still satisfies.
--- a/lifter/TraceManager.cpp
+++ b/lifter/TraceManager.cpp
@@ -184,10 +184,24 @@
           break;
         }
       }
-      std::stringstream fn_name;
-      fn_name << "fn_plt_" << std::hex << b_entry;
+      // Name a PLT stub the way every other lifted function is named.
+      //
+      // This path built the name by hand and skipped GetUniqueLiftedFuncName,
+      // so a stub came out `fn_plt_<hex>` while a symbol-derived function comes
+      // out `<sym>_____<hex>`. Nothing downstream parsed the difference until
+      // cross-program reuse did: builder/namespace-object decides which
+      // definitions may carry one name across the programs of a closure by
+      // reading the `_____<hex>` address out of the name, and a stub has no such
+      // address to read. Every stub was therefore tagged per program -- including
+      // the 442 of them that sit in LIBRARY sections at a closure-fixed address
+      // and are identical in every program. They reached 89 of 90 partitions.
+      //
+      // GetUniqueLiftedFuncName("fn_plt", b_entry) gives `fn_plt_____<hex>`.
+      // elfconv's own runtime matches these with strncmp(name, "fn_plt", 6),
+      // which is unaffected.
       disasm_funcs.emplace(b_entry,
-                           DisasmFunc(fn_name.str(), b_entry, (plt_section.vma + ins_i) - b_entry));
+                           DisasmFunc(GetUniqueLiftedFuncName("fn_plt", b_entry), b_entry,
+                                      (plt_section.vma + ins_i) - b_entry));
     }
   }
 
@@ -216,8 +230,18 @@
             /* nop */ _s_fn_bytes[i + 8] == 0x1f && _s_fn_bytes[i + 9] == 0x20 &&
             _s_fn_bytes[i + 10] == 0x03 && _s_fn_bytes[i + 11] == 0xd5) {
           uint64_t __wrap_main_vma = s_t_addrs[j] + i;
+          // Address-derived for the same reason as the PLT stubs above, but the
+          // consequence here is the opposite one: this wraps the guest's own
+          // `main`, so its body differs in every program. Left bare, it is a
+          // FUNCTION with no address in its name, and any rule that treats such
+          // functions as program-independent -- which they otherwise are, since
+          // remill's `extern "C"` glue is the only other source of them -- would
+          // share two different bodies under one name. Naming it by address puts
+          // it below the closure's first library base, where it is correctly
+          // kept per program.
           disasm_funcs.emplace(__wrap_main_vma,
-                               DisasmFunc("__wrap_main", __wrap_main_vma, __wrap_main_size));
+                               DisasmFunc(GetUniqueLiftedFuncName("__wrap_main", __wrap_main_vma),
+                                          __wrap_main_vma, __wrap_main_size));
           __wrap_main_found = true;
           goto found_entry;
         }
